-- gap[100] rung 4 — the world-receiver shape, worked and verified BY VALUE.
--
-- Rung 4 says `os` stops being reached ambiently and arrives as an OPERAND the
-- caller could have been refused. Enforcement (rung 5) is sema and is not
-- written yet, so this file does the part that does not need it: it shows the
-- shape working, so the 620-site conversion has an exemplar to copy instead of
-- a paragraph to interpret.
--
-- THE TEST THAT MATTERS is not "does it run" — an ambient call runs too. It is:
-- CAN THE CAPABILITY BE WITHHELD? A world is only a world if handing over a
-- different one changes what the callee can do, and handing over nothing stops
-- it entirely. Both directions are asserted below.
--
-- What this does NOT claim: it does not make `os` unreachable. Nothing here
-- prevents a callee from ignoring the operand and calling os.clock() directly,
-- because that refusal is rung 5 and lives in sema. This is the shape, ready
-- for the enforcement to make it the only shape.
--
-- exit 0 all good; 1..4 names the direction that broke.

-- A world is an ORDINARY VALUE. That is the whole point: no new grammar, no
-- new sigil, no capability keyword. It carries authority because of what is
-- inside it, and it is refusable because it is an argument.
realclock = { now = () os.clock() }

-- A world that answers, but with no authority behind it. This is what "refused"
-- looks like from the callee's side: same shape, no reach.
fakeclock = { now = () 0.0 }

-- The consumer names its need in its PARAMETER LIST. It cannot reach the clock
-- any other way, and a reader can see the authority it takes without reading
-- the body — which is the property ambient reach destroys.
elapsed = (w, since: f64): f64
    w.now() - since

main = (): i64
    -- direction 1: the real world works
    a = realclock.now()
    d = elapsed(realclock, a)
    if d < 0.0
        print("world proof: real clock ran backwards: {d}")
        std.os.exit(1)

    -- direction 2: THE CAPABILITY CAN BE WITHHELD. Same callee, same call, a
    -- world with no authority — and the answer changes. If this asserted equal
    -- values it would prove nothing: an ambient call would pass it too.
    z = elapsed(fakeclock, 1000.0)
    if z != -1000.0
        print("world proof: withheld clock still reached real time: {z}")
        std.os.exit(2)

    -- direction 3: the callee is HONEST about what it took. `elapsed` has two
    -- true arguments, and neither is a hidden ambient reach. A version that
    -- cheated would answer the same for both worlds.
    if elapsed(realclock, 0.0) == elapsed(fakeclock, 0.0)
        print("world proof: both worlds gave one answer — the operand is decorative")
        std.os.exit(3)

    print("world proof: authority arrives as an operand and CAN be withheld")
    0
